 and isolation produced by America’s prison system.","confidence":0.98},"academicOrientation":{"score":58,"confidence":0.86},"profileConfidence":0.93,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"020c3fd61265c6a06d6f263e8a28ae51f6b2639b13d979aba3eb327054e8c06b","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Arts & Criticism","secondarySubjects":["True Crime & Justice","Race & Ethnicity"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily focuses on the intersection of art and the experiences of incarcerated individuals, showcasing how they assert their humanity through creativity within the context of the American prison system.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"5659fc492872a31381d5600df416147fe9a9bed8382d6c23cd7275d1ae8f4d77","summary":"Winner of the National Book Critics Circle Award A Smithsonian Book of the Year A New York Review of Books “Best of 2020” Selection A New York Times Best Art Book of the Year An Art Newspaper Book of the Year A powerful document of the inner lives and creative visions of men and women rendered invisible by America’s prison system. More than two million people are currently behind bars in the United States. Incarceration not only separates the imprisoned from their families and communities; it also exposes them to shocking levels of deprivation and abuse and subjects them to the arbitrary cruelties of the criminal justice system. Yet, as Nicole Fleetwood reveals, America’s prisons are filled with art. Despite the isolation and degradation they experience, the incarcerated are driven to assert their humanity in the face of a system that dehumanizes them. Based on interviews with currently and formerly incarcerated artists, prison visits, and the author’s own family experiences with the penal system, Marking Time shows how the imprisoned turn ordinary objects into elaborate works of art. Working with meager supplies and in the harshest conditions—including solitary confinement—these artists find ways to resist the brutality and depravity that prisons engender. The impact of their art, Fleetwood observes, can be felt far beyond prison walls.","pageCount":350,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"Art","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-marking-time-art-in-the-age-of-mass-incarceration-nicole-r-fleetwood"}],"displaySummary":"The impact of their art, Fleetwood observes, can be felt far beyond prison walls.","primaryTopic":"Prisons & Incarceration","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Arts & Criticism","confidence":"high","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Arts & Criticism","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Arts & Criticism","mappedSubject":"Arts & Criticism","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily focuses on the intersection of art and the experiences of incarcerated individuals, showcasing how they assert their humanity through creativity within the context of the American prison system.","id":"subject-evidence-book-marking-time-art-in-the-age-of-mass-incarceration-nicole-r-fleetwood-1"},{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"Art","mappedSubject":"Arts & Criticism","score":5,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-marking-time-art-in-the-age-of-mass-incarceration-nicole-r-fleetwood","id":"subject-evidence-book-marking-time-art-in-the-age-of-mass-incarceration-nicole-r-fleetwood-4"},{"source":"keyword_classifier","rawLabel":"American History","mappedSubject":"American History","score":2,"confidence":"low","note":"American history keyword; society/culture keyword; arts/criticism keyword; gender/sexuality keyword; crime/justice keyword","id":"subject-evidence-book-marking-time-art-in-the-age-of-mass-incarceration-nicole-r-fleetwood-5"},{"source":"keyword_classifier","rawLabel":"Arts & Criticism","mappedSubject":"Arts & Criticism","score":2,"co